@* Components/AppStepItem.razor — one step in AppStepper (CLAUDE.md §3.1,
   Figma component "Stepper btn"). Native <button>, not a MudBlazor wrapper:
   MudBlazor 8.7.0 does ship MudStep/MudStepper, but that component's model
   is a linear/non-linear WIZARD — connected steps driving a single
   switching content panel, with a built-in Next/Previous/Complete action
   bar (verified against the installed MudBlazor.xml docs, not assumed).
   This screen's stepper is a page-level nav strip with no content-panel
   switching and no wizard action bar (confirmed against the real usage in
   demo/ScreenB/TaskTwo_from_figma_design.png) — forcing that layout into
   MudStepper would mean hiding almost all of its own built-in chrome,
   which is closer to "restructuring a MudBlazor component" than styling
   one (CLAUDE.md rule 7). A small custom component, same pattern as
   AppButton/AppStatusCard, fits without fighting anything.

   Disabled uses the real disabled attribute (native semantic HTML first,
   CLAUDE.md §6): a disabled <button> is already excluded from the tab
   order and announced as unavailable by assistive tech, so no extra
   aria-disabled is layered on top of it. cursor:not-allowed is added in
   CSS since the UA default for a disabled button is just cursor:default,
   which doesn't by itself communicate "locked" the way CLAUDE.md §6 asks.

   The icon is aria-hidden: the button's accessible name comes from its own
   text content, which is exactly the label span below — marking the icon
   hidden avoids the icon's own glyph name (e.g. "Info") being announced
   alongside the real step title.

   The label span is the ONE place the step title lives, always rendered as
   real text on every breakpoint (never display:none) — CLAUDE.md §6 (a
   step's meaning must survive when the label is hidden on mobile). Desktop
   shows it normally; responsive-rtl.css visually clips it below 600px
   using the exact same technique already established in
   AppHeader.razor.css for its own sync-button label (Bootstrap's
   .visually-hidden equivalent, written out by hand since it needs to apply
   conditionally by breakpoint) — reused, not reinvented. *@

<li class="app-stepper__item">
    <button type="button"
            class="@ItemClass"
            disabled="@(State == AppStepState.Disabled)"
            aria-current="@(State == AppStepState.Active ? "step" : null)"
            @onclick="HandleClick">
        <span class="app-stepper__icon" aria-hidden="true">
            <MudIcon Icon="@Icon" Color="Color.Inherit" />
        </span>
        <span class="@LabelClass">@Label</span>
    </button>
</li>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Icon { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Label { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    /// See AppStepDefinition.AlwaysShowLabel (Components/AppStepDefinition.cs)
    /// for why this exists — opts THIS step's label out of the icon-only
    /// mobile treatment in responsive-rtl.css. Default false keeps every
    /// step's markup byte-for-byte identical to before this parameter
    /// existed.
    [Parameter] public bool AlwaysShowLabel { get; set; }

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public AppStepState State { get; set; }

    /// Fired for Pass/Selected/Active (every state except Disabled — the
    /// native disabled attribute above already blocks the click for that
    /// one, so no extra guard is needed here).
    [Parameter] public EventCallback OnClick { get; set; }

    private string LabelClass => AlwaysShowLabel
        ? "app-stepper__label app-stepper__label--always-visible"
        : "app-stepper__label";

    private string ItemClass => State switch
    {
        AppStepState.Disabled => "app-stepper__btn app-stepper__btn--disabled",
        AppStepState.Selected => "app-stepper__btn app-stepper__btn--selected",
        AppStepState.Pass => "app-stepper__btn app-stepper__btn--pass",
        AppStepState.Active => "app-stepper__btn app-stepper__btn--active",
        _ => "app-stepper__btn",
    };

    private Task HandleClick() => OnClick.HasDelegate ? OnClick.InvokeAsync() : Task.CompletedTask;
}
